WEBVTT

00:00:00.267 --> 00:00:02.900
Last clip you learned to promote
a change with confidence.

00:00:02.900 --> 00:00:05.267
Now the change that reaches a real inbox.

00:00:05.267 --> 00:00:08.533
One real message leaves your system,
a second person has approved it,

00:00:08.533 --> 00:00:10.900
and every other engine stays on Dry Mode.

00:00:10.967 --> 00:00:14.567
Over the next few minutes you'll
have a live sender, an active lane,

00:00:14.567 --> 00:00:16.433
and only Outreach set to Live.

00:00:16.467 --> 00:00:20.733
Most teams flip one switch and hope.
Mia turns Dry Mode off on a Friday,

00:00:20.733 --> 00:00:23.200
and every engine that inherits
from it goes live with it.

00:00:23.200 --> 00:00:26.867
By Monday, a stranger has a cold
email with a number nobody can prove.

00:00:26.867 --> 00:00:28.100
Order prevents that.

00:00:28.167 --> 00:00:31.300
Step one, ask whether you're
ready. The check is read only.

00:00:31.300 --> 00:00:34.167
It covers live mode,
readiness, your lanes,

00:00:34.167 --> 00:00:36.600
and whether definitions
are signed and promoted.

00:00:36.600 --> 00:00:38.333
In Claude Code, type /go-live-check.

00:00:38.333 --> 00:00:40.700
In the console: Integrations, Connections.

00:00:40.700 --> 00:00:43.433
Or ask your agent: "Run
the go live check".

00:00:43.467 --> 00:00:44.467
Now the sender.

00:00:44.467 --> 00:00:48.600
Outreach needs a real mailbox bound to
the lane, so caps apply per mailbox.

00:00:48.600 --> 00:00:52.733
In the console: Settings, Connected
accounts, with the send feature on.

00:00:52.733 --> 00:00:56.200
Or ask your agent: "Which sender
is live and which lane uses it".

00:00:56.200 --> 00:00:59.800
The sign in is yours, because an agent
can't click through that consent.

00:00:59.800 --> 00:01:04.300
In live mode a fixture sender is refused,
so a fake success can't fool you.

00:01:04.333 --> 00:01:05.733
Step two, activate a lane.

00:01:05.733 --> 00:01:09.833
A lane is the guardrail around
sending: channel, daily cap,

00:01:09.833 --> 00:01:11.767
touch window and sending domain.

00:01:11.767 --> 00:01:16.867
Activation is a proposal, so a different
human approves it, and Sophia does,

00:01:16.867 --> 00:01:20.933
never the proposer. In the console:
Engines, Outreach, lanes, Activate.

00:01:20.933 --> 00:01:26.200
Or ask your agent: "Activate lane
primary-email in prod". Watch this one.

00:01:26.200 --> 00:01:30.600
With a sending domain, activation
refuses until SPF, DKIM and DMARC verify.

00:01:30.667 --> 00:01:33.133
Step three, rehearse
with Dry Mode still on,

00:01:33.133 --> 00:01:36.100
by proposing a first touch
to a teammate anyway.

00:01:36.100 --> 00:01:39.467
You get a logged draft, nothing
sends, and no approval exists.

00:01:39.467 --> 00:01:42.967
In the console: the Outreach
workbench, then Operate, Actions.

00:01:42.967 --> 00:01:43.867
Or ask your agent:

00:01:43.867 --> 00:01:45.833
"Draft a first touch to our internal

00:01:45.833 --> 00:01:48.767
test contact on lane
primary-email and propose it".

00:01:48.800 --> 00:01:50.633
Read the claims check in that draft.

00:01:50.633 --> 00:01:53.933
Every factual claim is checked
against active proof twice,

00:01:53.933 --> 00:01:57.400
when the action is proposed
and again when it's sent.

00:01:57.400 --> 00:02:00.033
Each claim comes back
backed, close, or no proof.

00:02:00.033 --> 00:02:03.333
Close never counts as backed,
and no proof blocks the send.

00:02:03.400 --> 00:02:04.933
Step four starts with guardrails,

00:02:04.933 --> 00:02:06.967
which apply to every
engine and every agent.

00:02:06.967 --> 00:02:09.167
Set emails a day, new accounts a week,

00:02:09.167 --> 00:02:11.633
and a send window in the
recipient's timezone.

00:02:11.633 --> 00:02:14.733
A hit doesn't drop the action.
It holds it, then runs it later.

00:02:14.733 --> 00:02:16.733
In the console: Settings, Safety.

00:02:16.733 --> 00:02:19.567
Or ask your agent: "Show my
guardrails and today's usage".

00:02:19.567 --> 00:02:21.700
Pro move: set them tight for week one.

00:02:21.733 --> 00:02:24.133
Now one engine live.
Dry Mode has two layers,

00:02:24.133 --> 00:02:27.300
the organization switch and
a Live switch per engine,

00:02:27.300 --> 00:02:30.167
and the stricter one wins.
Check the engines card first:

00:02:30.167 --> 00:02:33.500
an engine that inherits from the
organization goes live with it,

00:02:33.500 --> 00:02:35.400
so set the others to Dry.

00:02:35.400 --> 00:02:37.633
Then an owner or admin
turns the organization

00:02:37.633 --> 00:02:39.733
switch off and sets only Outreach to Live.

00:02:39.733 --> 00:02:42.467
Both steps are human only,
confirmed and audited,

00:02:42.467 --> 00:02:44.600
so your agent points you to the screen.

00:02:44.600 --> 00:02:46.867
Leave autonomy and envelopes off for now.

00:02:46.900 --> 00:02:50.833
Step five, propose the internal send
again. This time an approval appears,

00:02:50.833 --> 00:02:54.100
and Sophia opens it to read the
frozen message and the claims panel.

00:02:54.100 --> 00:02:56.333
The approval is bound
to the exact payload,

00:02:56.333 --> 00:02:58.233
so what she approves is what goes out.

00:02:58.233 --> 00:03:00.367
Someone other than the
proposer executes it,

00:03:00.367 --> 00:03:04.400
and the message lands in a real inbox,
which proves the chain end to end.

00:03:04.467 --> 00:03:07.967
Now read what happened. In the console:
Outreach, then Operate, Monitors.

00:03:07.967 --> 00:03:09.000
Or ask your agent:

00:03:09.000 --> 00:03:12.700
"Show deliverability for lane
primary-email and the suppression rules".

00:03:12.700 --> 00:03:14.767
Monitors watch bounce and complaint rates,

00:03:14.767 --> 00:03:17.100
and can pause a lane when they spike.

00:03:17.100 --> 00:03:19.800
A paused lane blocks approved
sends until a person resumes it.

00:03:19.800 --> 00:03:22.533
One honest limit: bounce reports
from a connected mailbox,

00:03:22.533 --> 00:03:25.267
and delivery events from some providers,

00:03:25.267 --> 00:03:26.933
aren't checked against real accounts yet.

00:03:26.933 --> 00:03:29.267
Your first internal send
is where you check them.

00:03:29.333 --> 00:03:33.533
Who approves matters as much as
how. Have a second owner or admin.

00:03:33.533 --> 00:03:37.100
With only one, add another using
gtm member add and the admin role.

00:03:37.100 --> 00:03:40.000
Proposals expire after seven days,

00:03:40.000 --> 00:03:41.833
so a queue nobody reads
quietly blocks you.

00:03:41.833 --> 00:03:44.100
For the first few days, keep Outreach,

00:03:44.100 --> 00:03:46.667
Needs a human and the Actions ledger open.

00:03:46.667 --> 00:03:49.833
If anything surprises you,
set the engine back to Dry,

00:03:49.833 --> 00:03:52.000
since going dry is always allowed.

00:03:52.033 --> 00:03:56.267
Then widen one step at a time: raise
the daily cap a notch, add a segment,

00:03:56.267 --> 00:03:59.533
then add a second engine
set to Live on its own.

00:03:59.533 --> 00:04:03.567
Every step repeats the pattern:
draft in Dry, read, approve, go live.

00:04:03.633 --> 00:04:07.133
The objection: one slip, and a
bad email reaches a real customer.

00:04:07.133 --> 00:04:08.300
Here's what has to line up.

00:04:08.300 --> 00:04:11.400
A first message to a new person
always waits for a human.

00:04:11.400 --> 00:04:14.767
No proof means a blocked claim.
Any cap hit holds the action.

00:04:14.767 --> 00:04:16.167
Only Outreach is live,

00:04:16.167 --> 00:04:19.033
and the person who executes
isn't the person who proposed.

00:04:19.067 --> 00:04:22.567
Keep these: Check readiness and
connect a sender. Activate a lane.

00:04:22.567 --> 00:04:25.633
Rehearse in Dry Mode. Then
take Outreach live alone,

00:04:25.633 --> 00:04:27.867
with a second person
approving the first send.

00:04:27.900 --> 00:04:31.933
One lane, one engine, one real send,
and a second person who approved it.

00:04:31.933 --> 00:04:33.967
Keep the Actions ledger open for a week.

00:04:33.967 --> 00:04:37.167
Up next, Teams, roles and
seats: who gets which reach,

00:04:37.167 --> 00:04:38.900
and how your agents inherit it.
